---
description: Enforce the System-First UI/UX workflow, user approval gates, approved design tokens, scope fidelity, and independent review.
alwaysApply: false
---

When asked to start or continue UI/UX Design:

1. Read `docs/process/DEVELOPMENT_WORKFLOW.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and `docs/process/UI_UX_DESIGN.md` in full, plus approved PRD, Technical Design, Implementation Plan, decision records, and relevant repository evidence.
2. Keep the primary session as the sole user-facing UI/UX Designer.
3. Follow System-First order and stop at each user approval gate: structural baseline; Visual Direction; Design Token/core-component baseline; final UI/UX approval.
4. Record each direction's reference source. Adopt observable characteristics only. Do not copy that source's branding or infer unapproved features, themes, screens, or behavior. The user approves an openable primary-screen page; extract tokens from that page.
5. Do not set final token values before Visual Direction approval. Do not scale high-fidelity design before token/component approval.
6. Use approved semantic/component tokens and component specifications. Record justified exceptions; do not introduce arbitrary page-level styling.
7. Maintain PRD AC → flow → screen → component/interaction traceability.
8. Preserve approved scope and technical contracts. Do not write application code, install dependencies, or directly edit Implementation Plan.
9. At the Design Review Gate, delegate to `.cursor/agents/ui-ux-design-reviewer.md` with read-only/tool restrictions where actually supported. Reviewer returns findings only; primary agent triages findings and asks user about product decisions.
10. Disclose unavailable native delegation or tool-level sandboxing. Self-check is not independent review.
11. Stage 6 compares the design package with the Gate 1 page. Runtime screenshot comparison with that same page requires implementation authorization and belongs to Coding & Testing.
12. UI/UX approval does not authorize coding; wait for separate explicit authorization.
